  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft will maintain the hazy, very warm, and dry \nconditions for the next 24 hours or so. Shortwave energy and \ninstability capable of initiating shower and thunderstorm activity\nlooks to remain north of the Canadian border, though some \nisolated storms may begin to creep towards the Hi-Line this \nevening and tonight. Seasonably breezy conditions will continue \nwith wind gusts exceeding 20 mph at times.\n\nA Pacific trough begins to move southeastward through BC/AB and \ninto the Northern Rockies late Wednesday through Friday. Ensembles\nremain in agreement with temperatures falling below average \nThursday and Friday, but still differ on how widespread the \nrainfall will be. The Wednesday night through Thursday time period\nis generally favored to see the most widespread rainfall. While \nsome areas may see beneficial rains and cooling temperatures, the \nconvective nature of this activity may also bring a few robust \nstorms with localized strong gusty winds, hail, and heavy \ndownpours capable of flooding of normally flood prone areas. ML \nCAPE values will initially run in 200 to 500 J/kg range on \nWednesday before rising to around 300 to 900 J/kg on Thursday when\nconvective activity looks to peak. It begins to decrease again on\nFriday with showers and thunderstorms retreating to southwest MT.\n\nRidging aloft begins to build back in heading into the weekend, \nbut another trough approaching from the Pacific NW will bring \nsouthwesterly flow aloft back to the Northern Rockies. This will \nresult in temperatures warming back up, along with daily scattered\nshower and thunderstorm activity. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nBreezy conditions, shifting winds, and localized elevated to \ncritical fire concerns...\n\nThe combination of very warm, dry, and breezy conditions will \nresult in localized elevated to critical fire weather conditions \nfor another day. Surface boundaries and or diurnal mixing will \nbring wind shifts and gusts in excess of 20 mph at times. \nAfternoon humidity will fall to or near critically low levels with\nonly moderate to poor overnight recoveries. The hottest, driest, \nand breeziest conditions will be focused over southwest MT where a\nred flag warning is in effect today. Those working to contain \nongoing fires should continue be mindful of the hot, dry and \nbreezy conditions. \n\nCooler temperatures, some stronger storms, and beneficial rains \nfor some Wednesday through Friday...\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in agreement with cooler and wetter \nconditions moving in late Wednesday through Friday. Climate \nanomaly indices, including the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index are \nshowing signals for precipitation amounts above climatology for \nthis time of year. While this rainfall is definitely welcome, \nthere are some caveats to consider. The convective nature of this \nprecipitation will result in uneven distribution and increased \ninstances of stronger storms capable of localized strong wind \ngusts, hail, and even flooding of normally prone areas. At this \ntime, the most widespread precipitation looks to occur on \nThursday. \n\nScattered gusty thunderstorms this weekend...\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft returning to the Northern Rockies will \nmaintain mid-level moisture and shortwave energy this weekend \nwhile the surface boundary layer warms up and dries out. This will\nresult in scattered showers and thunderstorms for both days, \nmostly in the afternoon and evening. The primary hazards will be \nlocalized strong, gusty winds, and cloud to ground lightning. \n- RCG",
